<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godfrey-of-bouillon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Godfrey of Bouillon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godfrey of Bouillon was Duke of Lower Lorraine and a leader of the First Crusade, son of Count Eustace II of Boulogne.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godin-tepe"><span class="enc-name">Godin Tepe</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Godin Tepe is an archaeological site in the Kangavar valley of western Iran, on the route through the Zagros Mountains towards Mesopotamia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/godomar-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godomar-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Godomar II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godomar II was the last king of the Burgundians, reigning from 524 to 534.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godred-crovan"><span class="enc-name">Godred Crovan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godred Crovan was a Norse-Gaelic warrior who, after surviving the Battle of Stamford Bridge in 1066, seized the Isle of Man in 1079 following a battle at Sky Hill.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godred-olafsson"><span class="enc-name">Godred Olafsson</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godred Olafsson was king of Mann and the Isles from 1153 to 1187, son of Olaf I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/godwin-friday"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/godwin-friday.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Godwin Friday</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godwin Friday is a Vincentian politician who became prime minister of Saint Vincent and the Grenadines in November 2025.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/godwin-earl-of-wessex"><span class="enc-name">Godwin, Earl of Wessex</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Godwin was an Anglo-Saxon nobleman who rose under Cnut to become Earl of Wessex and the most powerful man in England after the king.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/goejanverwellesluis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/goejanverwellesluis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Goejanverwellesluis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Goejanverwellesluis is a lock and hamlet on the Hollandse IJssel near Gouda, in South Holland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gogugwon-of-goguryeo"><span class="enc-name">Gogugwon of Goguryeo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gogugwon of Goguryeo was the sixteenth king of Goguryeo, reigning from 331 to 371.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/goguryeo-sui-war"><span class="enc-name">Goguryeo–Sui War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 598–614</span><span class="enc-snip">The Goguryeo–Sui War was a series of failed invasions of the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo by the Sui dynasty in the early seventh century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/goh-chok-tong"><span class="enc-name">Goh Chok Tong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Goh Chok Tong (born 1941) is a Singaporean politician who served as the second prime minister of Singapore from 1990 to 2004, succeeding Lee Kuan Yew.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gohad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gohad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height